<article class="env-lemma" id="052T">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/052T">Lemma <span data-tag="052T">10.70.11</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $I \subset R$ be an ideal. Let $a \in I$. If $a$ is not contained in any minimal prime of $R$, then $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R[\frac{I}{a}]) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ has dense image. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $a^ k x = 0$ for $x \in R$, then $x$ is contained in all the minimal primes of $R$ and hence nilpotent, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00E0" data-tag="00E0">10.17.2</a>. Thus the kernel of $R \to R[\frac{I}{a}]$ consists of nilpotent elements. Hence the result follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/00FL" data-tag="00FL">10.30.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>